1. Recommended End-to-End Architecture

I would divide the solution into six platform layers.

Layer 1 — Source
Approved Base AMI
       ↓
Amazon Linux
RHEL
Windows Server
Ubuntu

Only approved base images should enter the pipeline.

The base AMI should be tracked by:

OS
OS version
Architecture
Publisher
AMI ID
Release date
EOL date
2. Image Build

Use AWS Image Builder as the central image-building platform.

AWS Image Builder
       |
       +── Build Components
       |
       +── Test Components
       |
       +── Image Pipeline
       |
       +── Distribution Configuration
       |
       └── AMI

Instead of an engineer manually creating an EC2 VM, installing software, patching it and creating an AMI, Image Builder performs the process consistently.

3. Hardening

This should happen automatically during the image build.

Base AMI
   ↓
CIS Baseline
   ↓
Security Configuration
   ↓
Enterprise Configuration

Examples:

Linux
SSH configuration
Password policy
PAM
File permissions
Unnecessary services
Firewall
Audit configuration
Windows
Password policy
Local security policy
Windows Firewall
RDP configuration
Unnecessary services
Security auditing
PowerShell logging

The important platform-engineering principle is:

Don't manually harden every EC2 instance. Harden the image once and deploy from the image.

4. Patching

Integrate Systems Manager Patch Manager into the image lifecycle.

Image Builder
     ↓
Launch Temporary Build Instance
     ↓
SSM Agent
     ↓
Patch Manager
     ↓
Approved Patch Baseline
     ↓
Install Security Updates
     ↓
Reboot if Required
     ↓
Validation

This gives you a predictable patch level before an AMI reaches production.

5. SSM Automation

Use SSM Automation Documents for configuration that needs to be applied consistently.

For example:

SSM Automation
      |
      +── Install CloudWatch Agent
      |
      +── Install Security Agent
      |
      +── Configure Logging
      |
      +── Configure Monitoring
      |
      +── Configure Enterprise Settings
      |
      +── Validate Services

This is particularly useful when the same configuration needs to be applied to Windows and Linux images with OS-specific automation.

6. Security Validation

This is a critical part of the architecture.

Do not publish an AMI just because Image Builder successfully completed.

The pipeline should perform validation.

