7. Approval Gate

For production images, I would introduce an approval gate.

Image Build
    ↓
Security Validation
    ↓
     PASS
      ↓
Approval
 ┌────┴────┐
 │         │
Security  Platform
 │         │
 └────┬────┘
      ↓
Publish AMI

For DEV environments, this could potentially be automated.

For production:

Automated validation → human approval → production release

This gives you governance without making the entire process manual.

8. AMI Versioning

Don't use generic AMI names such as:

Windows-Golden-AMI

Use predictable versioning.

For example:

VDOT-Windows-2019-CIS-L1-2026.09.001
VDOT-Windows-2022-CIS-L1-2026.09.001
VDOT-RHEL-9-CIS-L1-2026.09.001

You can maintain metadata such as:

Attribute	Example
OS	Windows Server 2022
CIS Level	Level 1
CIS Version	3.x
Patch Month	September 2026
AMI Version	2026.09.001
Build Date	25-Sep-2026
Image Builder Pipeline	Windows-2022
Security Scan	Passed
Vulnerability Scan	Passed
Approved By	Platform/Security
9. Terraform Integration

This is where the architecture becomes significantly better.

Terraform should not create an AMI.

Terraform should consume the approved AMI.

Example:

                    AMI Pipeline
                         |
                         ▼
                  Approved AMI
                         |
                         ▼
                 AMI ID / Parameter
                         |
                         ▼
                 Terraform Module
                         |
             +-----------+-----------+
             |                       |
             ▼                       ▼
        Launch Template          EC2
             |
             ▼
       Auto Scaling Group

Your Terraform code could retrieve the approved AMI from SSM Parameter Store.

For example:

/aws/service/company/golden-ami/windows-2022

Then Terraform uses that AMI ID.

This prevents developers from manually selecting arbitrary AMIs.

